== 2. Of the Household Service of God

A family, for its own good state, is bound to perform two duties: one to God, the other to itself.

The duty to God is the private worship and service of God, which must be established and settled in every family. And the reasons for this are these.

First, because this duty stands by the express commandment of God, who by his apostle says, “I want the men everywhere to pray, lifting up holy hands, without anger or dissension” (1 Tim 2:8).

#emph[Again, it is confirmed by the custom and practice of holy men in their times.] “For I know him,” says the Lord concerning Abraham, “that he will command his children and his household after him to keep the way of the #smallcaps[Lord] by doing what is right and just, in order that the #smallcaps[Lord] may bring upon Abraham what He has promised” (Gen 18:19). To the same purpose Joshua speaks: “But if it is unpleasing in your#super[+] sight to serve the #smallcaps[Lord], then choose for yourselves#super[+] this day whom you#super[+] will serve, whether the gods your#super[+] fathers served beyond the Euphrates, or the gods of the Amorites in whose land you#super[+] are living. As for me and my house, we will serve the #smallcaps[Lord]!” (Josh 24:15). And Cornelius is reported to have been such a man: “He and all his household were devout and God-fearing” (Acts 10:2).

#emph[Thirdly, common reason and equity show it to be a necessary duty. For the happy and prosperous state of the family, which consists in the mutual love and agreement of the husband and wife, in the dutiful obedience of children to their parents, and in the faithful service of servants to their masters, depends wholly on the grace and blessing of God; and this blessing is joined to his worship.] For 1 Tim 4:8: “Godliness is valuable in every way, holding promise for the present life and for the one to come.” Ps 127:1: “Unless the #smallcaps[Lord] builds the house, its builders labor in vain; unless the #smallcaps[Lord] protects the city, its watchmen stand guard in vain.” v. 2: “In vain you#super[+] rise early and stay up late, toiling for bread to eat—for He gives sleep to His beloved.” v. 3: “Children are indeed a heritage from the #smallcaps[Lord], and the fruit of the womb is His reward.” Ps 128:1: “Blessed are all who fear the #smallcaps[Lord], who walk in His ways!” v. 2: “For when you eat the fruit of your labor, blessings and prosperity will be yours.” v. 3: “Your wife will be like a fruitful vine flourishing within your house, your children like olive shoots sitting around your table.” v. 6: “May you see your children’s children. Peace be upon Israel!” 1 Sam 1:27: “I prayed for this boy,” says Hannah, “and the #smallcaps[Lord] has granted me what I asked of Him.”

#emph[The household service of God has two parts. The first is talking together on the word of God, for the edification of all the members of the family to eternal life. The second is calling on the name of God, with giving thanks for his benefits. Both of these are commended in the Scriptures.] Deut 6:6: “These words I am commanding you today are to be upon your heart.” v. 7: “And you shall teach them diligently to your children and speak of them when you sit at home and when you walk along the road, when you lie down and when you get up.” v. 20: “In the future, when your son asks, ‘What is the meaning of the decrees and statutes and ordinances that the #smallcaps[Lord] our God has commanded you#super[+]?’” v. 21: “Then you are to tell him, ‘We were slaves of Pharaoh in Egypt, but the #smallcaps[Lord] brought us out of Egypt with a mighty hand.’” v. 24: “And the #smallcaps[Lord] commanded us to observe all these statutes and to fear the #smallcaps[Lord] our God.” Ps 14:1: “The fool says in his heart, ‘There is no God.’” v. 4: “They do not call upon the #smallcaps[Lord].”

The times of this service are these:

The morning, in which the family, coming together in one place, is to call on the name of the Lord before they begin the works of their callings.

The evening also is another time to be used in prayer. For the family has seen the blessing of God on their labors through the day, and now the time of rest draws on, in which every one is to commend his body and soul into the protection of the Lord. For no man knows what will befall him before he rises again; nor does anyone know whether he will ever rise again or not. It is therefore a reckless boldness to go to rest without praying.

Besides these, there are other times also in which to perform this duty, as before and after meals. For food and drink are blessed to those who receive them by the word and prayer. 1 Tim 4:4: “For every creation of God is good, and nothing that is received with thanksgiving should be rejected, because it is sanctified by the word of God and prayer.”

#emph[Now the word of God approves these times.] Deut 6:7: “You shall … speak of them … when you lie down and when you get up.” Ps 55:17: “Evening, morning, and noon I pray and cry out, and He hears my voice.” Ps 127:2: “In vain you#super[+] rise early and stay up late, toiling for bread to eat,” #emph[unless the Lord grants a blessing,] “for He gives sleep to His beloved.”

#emph[To these may at times be added the exercise of fasting, which is to be used, as occasion requires, both publicly and privately; especially when those of the family are called to it by some present or impending calamity.] Esth 4:16: “Go and assemble all the Jews who can be found in Susa, and fast for me. Do not eat or drink for three days, night or day, and I and my maidens will fast as you#super[+] do. After that, I will go to the king, even though it is against the law. And if I perish, I perish!” Zech 12:11: “On that day the wailing in Jerusalem will be great … The land will mourn, each family on its own: the family of the house of David on its own, and their wives on their own; the family of the house of Nathan on its own, and their wives on their own,” etc.

Families in which this service of God is performed are therefore, as it were, little churches, yes, even a kind of paradise on earth. For this reason St. Paul, writing to Philemon, greets “the church that meets at your house” (Phlm 2). And in the same way he sends greetings to the church of Corinth from Aquila and Priscilla, “and so does the church that meets at their house” (1 Cor 16:19). On the other side, where this duty of God’s service is not practiced, but is either for the most part or altogether neglected, a man may call those families no better than companies of profane and godless atheists. As they deny God in their hearts, so they are known by this mark, that “they do not call upon the #smallcaps[Lord]” (Ps 14:4). Yes, such families are fitly compared to a herd of swine, always feeding greedily on the acorns, but never looking up to the hand that beats them down, nor to the tree they fall from.

The other duty, which concerns the house itself, is that every member of the family, according to their ability, employ themselves in some honorable and profitable business, to maintain the temporal state and life of the whole. Gen 3:19: “By the sweat of your brow you will eat your bread, until you return to the ground—because out of it were you taken. For dust you are, and to dust you shall return.” 1 Cor 7:20: “Each one should remain in the calling he had when he was called.” Now if anyone in the family turns aside from his calling and entices another to idolatry, he is to be exposed (Deut 13:6). And again, their case is very fearful who do nothing in their houses but dress finely and dine daintily from day to day. This is the black mark that Scripture sets on the rich glutton, Luke 16:19: “Now there was a rich man dressed in purple and fine linen, who feasted sumptuously every day.”
